// CANDIDATE BOTTLENECKS TO CHECK, IN ORDER, WITH DIRECT EVIDENCE
// FOR EACH - never simply assume which one is responsible

// 1. CPU - check actual utilization during peak load
// top / htop output during the load test's plateau: CPU at 35%
// CONCLUSION: CPU is NOT the bottleneck - genuine headroom remains

// 2. Database connection pool - check actual pool utilization
const pool = new Pool({ max: 100 }); // the CONFIGURED maximum
// Monitoring pool.totalCount and pool.idleCount during the plateau:
// totalCount: 100, idleCount: 0 - ALL 100 connections are in use,
// with requests genuinely WAITING for one to free up
// CONCLUSION: THIS IS THE BOTTLENECK - confirmed by direct evidence,
// not assumption

// 3. Network bandwidth - check actual throughput vs available capacity
// Server's network interface: using 40 Mbps of a 1000 Mbps link
// CONCLUSION: network is NOT the bottleneck - substantial headroom

// THE FIX, directly targeting the CONFIRMED bottleneck specifically -
// increasing the pool size, since CPU and network both have genuine
// headroom to support more concurrent database work:
const pool = new Pool({ max: 100 }); // BEFORE
const pool = new Pool({ max: 300 }); // AFTER - increased specifically
  // because evidence confirmed this exact resource was the constraint

// RE-TEST: at 1,000 concurrent users, throughput now reaches
// 9,200 req/s with p95 latency back down to 70ms - CONFIRMED, the
// targeted fix genuinely resolved the actual, evidence-identified bottleneck

