// feature genuinely needs to handle 50,000 req/s during sales
// events, while the ADMIN/INVENTORY-MANAGEMENT feature genuinely
// never exceeds 50 req/s, even during the same sales events

// Lesson 3 (scaling strategy): horizontally scaling the ENTIRE
// monolith to handle the catalog's peak load means running 200
// instances of the WHOLE application, including the admin feature
// that never needed more than a handful - genuine, real waste

// Lesson 4 (load balancing): a single load balancer currently
// distributes ALL traffic uniformly across these 200 wastefully-
// duplicated instances, with no way to scale catalog and admin
// independently within a single monolithic deployment

// Lesson 5 (caching): the catalog feature would genuinely benefit
// from aggressive, catalog-specific caching, while admin's genuinely
// low traffic and need for immediate consistency makes caching
// actively undesirable there - these are GENUINELY DIFFERENT,
// even conflicting, architectural needs

// CONCLUSION: this SPECIFIC evidence (dramatically different,
// independently-varying scaling needs for catalog versus admin)
// genuinely justifies extracting the PRODUCT CATALOG into its own,
// independently-scalable microservice specifically - NOT a wholesale,
// unjustified split of EVERY feature into separate services,
// directly matching only the genuine, evidence-identified need

